  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k-2{t}^{3}$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product is equal to zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(k-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
